course: chinese-street
lesson: 29
title: Talking to the friend's grandparents
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ming's yéye and nǎinai (grandad and granny) live with his family, and to them you are a guest child to be fussed over. Greet them first, before any toy: «yéye nǎinai hǎo!», and use the respectful nín, not nǐ.
explain: Grandparents say the same few things on every visit: nǐ zhǎng gāo le! (you've grown so tall!), zhēn guāi! (such a good child!) and, at goodbye, hǎohāo xuéxí! (study hard!). Smile and say xièxie or hǎo de — don't argue.
explain: Some things sound odd to an English ear. «nǐ shòu le» (you've got thin!) is worry, and a full plate will follow. «nǐ pàng le» (you've put on weight!) from a Chinese granny often means «you look healthy» — don't be hurt, and never say it back.
explain: At the table nobody starts until the eldest does, so you say «nín xiān chī» (you start first). And if Granny speaks fast, or in Cantonese or Hokkien, «qǐng nín màn diǎnr shuō» asks her kindly to slow down.
fact: yéye nǎinai hǎo («YEH-yeh NIGH-nigh HOW») means hello Grandad and Granny, said to the friend's grandparents before anything else; they may beam and say zhēn guāi!
fact: nín shēntǐ hǎo ma («neen SHUN-tee HOW mah», literally «is your body well?») means are you keeping well; asking an elder about health is caring, not nosy.
fact: nín xiān chī («neen SHYEN CHR») means please eat first; children don't start until the eldest picks up the chopsticks or invites them with chī ba, chī ba!
fact: nín zuò zhèr («neen DZWAW JAR») means please sit here; giving your seat to an elder on a sofa or a bus is expected, not extra-nice.
fact: wǒ lái bāng nín («waw LIGH BAHNG neen») means let me help you; elders refuse once to be polite (bú yòng, bú yòng!), so offer again and help anyway.
fact: nǐ zhǎng gāo le («nee JAHNG GOW luh») means you've grown so tall, the Chinese «look at you!» from every relative at every visit; answer xièxie nǎinai.
fact: zhēn guāi («JUN GWIGH») means such a good child; guāi means well-behaved, and it is the highest praise from a granny.
fact: hǎohāo xuéxí («HOW-how SHWEH-shee») means study hard, said by elders especially at goodbye and New Year; the answer is hǎo de, nǎinai!
fact: qǐng nín màn diǎnr shuō («ching neen MAHN DYAR SHWAW») means please could you speak more slowly; many grandparents speak Cantonese, Hokkien or another dialect, which the friend may not fully understand either.
fact: nǐ shòu le («nee SHOW luh») means you've got thin; it is worry, not a compliment, and the answer is wǒ hěn hǎo! (I'm fine).
fact: nǐ pàng le («nee PAHNG luh») means you've put on weight; rude in English, from a Chinese granny it often means you look healthy and well-fed, and a child never says it back.
fact: lǎorénjiā («LAOW-run-JYAH») is a respectful word for an elder, as in tā lǎorénjiā shēntǐ hǎo; calling an elder just lǎo de (the old one) is rude.
exercise: You arrive and Ming's grandparents are on the sofa. What do you say first? | yéye nǎinai hǎo | nǐ pàng le | hǎohāo xuéxí | lǎo de hǎo
exercise: What does nín shēntǐ hǎo ma literally ask? | Is your body well? | Is your dinner ready? | Is your house very big? | Is your grandson good?
exercise: Is asking Granny about her health nosy? | No, it's a caring question to elders | Yes, it's very rude to ask any old person | Yes, only doctors ask that | No, but only at New Year
exercise: Dinner is on the table and Grandad hasn't started. What do you say? | nín xiān chī, you start first | chī ba, I'm starting, I'm starving | kuài diǎnr, hurry up | wǒ chī bǎo le, I'm full
exercise: When may a child start eating at a Chinese family table? | When the eldest starts or invites them | As soon as the food arrives on the table | After everyone else has finished eating | Whenever the child happens to feel hungry
exercise: The bus is full and an old man gets on. What do you say as you stand? | nín zuò zhèr | nǐ shòu le | zhēn guāi | màn zǒu
exercise: Granny carries heavy shopping. What do you offer? | wǒ lái bāng nín | nín xiān chī | hǎohāo xuéxí | nǐ zhǎng gāo le
exercise: Granny says «bú yòng, bú yòng!» when you offer to help. What should you do? | Offer again and help anyway | Walk away at once, she said no twice | Laugh at her | Ask Ming to do it
exercise: What does nǐ zhǎng gāo le mean? | You've grown tall! | You've grown so loud! | You've got so thin! | You're late for dinner!
exercise: Granny pats your head: «zhēn guāi!». What has she said? | Such a good child! | You're a naughty child! | You've grown so tall! | Your hands are so cold!
exercise: What does guāi mean? | Well-behaved | Clever | Strong | Funny
exercise: At goodbye Grandad says «hǎohāo xuéxí!». What's the right answer? | hǎo de, yéye! | bù xíng! | wǒ bù zhīdào | wǒ wàng le
exercise: Granny talks fast in a dialect and you're lost. What do you ask? | qǐng nín màn diǎnr shuō | qǐng nín xiān chī | nín zuò zhèr | wǒ lái bāng nín
exercise: Why might Ming not understand all his granny says, either? | She may speak Cantonese, Hokkien or another dialect | She speaks only English, with a very strong accent | She speaks backwards | She whispers
exercise: Granny says «nǐ shòu le!». What comes next? | Lots of food | A trip to the doctor | A telling-off | A quick goodbye
exercise: What does nǐ pàng le from a Chinese granny often mean? | You look healthy and well-fed | You must go on a diet, straight away | You're very rude | You're too tall
exercise: Which is a respectful word for an elder? | lǎorénjiā | lǎo de | xiǎo pìhái | bèndàn
test: «his nan said I got fat?! that's so rude» How should you take nǐ pàng le? | Don't be hurt: from a granny it means you look healthy | It's a real insult, so leave the flat at once | Say it back to be fair | It means you're tall
test: «oi granny nǐ pàng le too lol» What's wrong here? | A child never says it back to an elder | Nothing, it's a compliment, both ways round | It's only wrong in the morning | You should say it twice
test: «ming's grandad keeps saying study hard, every single time, so annoying» What's the best reply? | Just say hǎo de, don't argue | Tell him you already did all your homework | Say bù xíng | Ignore him
test: What would happen if you started eating before Ming's grandad? | It'd look rude; children wait for the eldest | Nothing, kids always eat first in Chinese homes | Grandad would be pleased | You'd get a red envelope
test: You stay in your seat on the bus while an old lady stands. How does it look in a Chinese family? | Rude: giving your seat is expected | Fine, seats are first come, first served | Polite, she likes standing | Normal, only adults give seats
test: «he said tā lǎo de is coming, that's how you say grandad?» Is it OK? | No, lǎo de is rude; say lǎorénjiā | Yes, it's the polite form for any grandad | Yes, it means Mr Old | No, you must say lǎoshī
test: In a cartoon a boy offers to carry Granny's bags, she says «bú yòng!», and he shrugs and goes. What would a good guest do? | Offer again and help anyway | Shrug and go, she said no | Take her bags without asking ever again | Tell her she's too old
test: «his granny said I got thin, then fed me like 4 bowls» Why? | nǐ shòu le is worry, so she fed you | She thought you were rich and could pay | It means you're greedy | It means go home now
test: «just say nǐ hǎo to grandparents, it's fine innit» What makes grannies beam more? | nín, the respectful «you»: yéye nǎinai hǎo | Saying hāi like English kids do | Saying nothing and waving | Shouting their names
test: «why does granny keep saying I got taller? I'm the same lol» What's she doing? | It's the usual «look at you!» greeting | She can't see well without her glasses | She's teasing you | She thinks you're someone else
test: Grandad says «chī ba, chī ba!» and picks up his chopsticks. What does it mean for you? | You may start eating | You must wait for Granny | You should leave the table | You must cook the rice
exam: Identify the expression whereby a child invites an elder to commence a meal before others. | nín xiān chī | nín zuò zhèr | hǎohāo xuéxí | nǐ zhǎng gāo le
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the remark nǐ pàng le when uttered by a Chinese grandparent. | It frequently signifies approval of a child's healthy appearance | It invariably constitutes a grave insult to the child and his family | It is an instruction to refrain from eating | It is a formula of farewell
exam: Ascertain the appropriate conduct where an elder initially declines an offer of assistance. | The offer is to be renewed and the help given | The offer is to be withdrawn forthwith | The matter is to be referred to the child's parents | The elder is to be reproached for refusing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term guāi? | It denotes good behaviour, a high commendation from an elder | It denotes great physical height | It denotes a dialect spoken by grandparents | It denotes a respectful term for an elderly person
